-- title:
--   Reflexivity, nonexpansive maps and the fixed point property
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals.
--
--   1. **Canonical reflexivity.** The canonical map $X\to X^{**}$ into the continuous bidual is surjective.
--   2. **Nonexpansive selfmaps.** For $C\subseteq X$, a map $F:C\to C$ is nonexpansive if
--   $$
--   \|F(a)-F(b)\|\le\|a-b\|\qquad(a,b\in C),
--   $$
--   measured in the given norm of $X$.
--   3. **Fixed point property.** $X$ has the nonexpansive fixed point property if every nonexpansive selfmap of every nonempty, closed, bounded, convex $C\subseteq X$ has a fixed point.
-- source:
